---
import ArticleLayout from '../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';
import type { ArticleMeta } from '../../types/article';
import { collectPosts } from '../../lib/posts';

export const meta: ArticleMeta = {
  title: 'How to Sell a Used PS5 for the Most Money',
  description:
    'A step-by-step guide to selling a used PS5 safely. Where to list it, how to price it, and the exact reset steps before you hand it over.',
  category: 'console',
  type: 'guide',
  keyword: 'how to sell a used ps5',
  date: '2026-09-19',
  updated: '2026-09-19',
  image: '/images/placeholder.svg',
  featured: false,
  affiliateUrl: 'https://www.amazon.com/s?k=playstation+5+console',
  author: 'Lucas Hart',
  authorBio:
    'Lucas Hart has spent five years testing PlayStation hardware, from hall effect controllers to SSD storage upgrades, and writes hands-on buying guides for Gearky.',
};

const related = collectPosts(import.meta.glob('./*.astro', { eager: true }))
  .filter((p) => p.title !== meta.title)
  .slice(0, 3);
---

<ArticleLayout
  meta={meta}
  related={related}
  faqs={[
    {
      q: 'How much is a used PS5 worth in 2026?',
      a: 'A clean, working disc model in good condition typically sells for roughly $300 to $400 privately, depending on included games, controllers, and box. Digital models sell for about $40 to $60 less.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Should I factory reset before selling?',
      a: 'Yes, always. A factory reset wipes your account and data from the console. Do it only after backing up saves and deactivating the console as your primary device.',
    },
    {
      q: 'Is it better to sell privately or trade in?',
      a: 'Private sale earns more, usually 30 to 40 percent more than a trade-in. A trade-in at GameStop or an online buyback service is instant but pays less.',
    },
    {
      q: 'What should I include when selling?',
      a: 'Include the HDMI cable, power cable, at least one controller, and the original box if you have it. The box alone can add $10 to $20 to your asking price.',
    },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    Selling a used PS5 is mostly about doing three things in order: protecting
    your account, pricing it right, and picking a place to sell that will not
    get you scammed. Get those three right and a clean disc model should pull
    $300 to $400 in the current market. Here is the full process, step by step.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 1: back up your saves</h2>

  <p>
    Your save data is the one thing you cannot get back after a reset. If you
    have PlayStation Plus, your saves are already syncing to the cloud. Check
    Settings, then Saved Data, to confirm the latest uploads went through. If
    you do not have Plus, copy your saves to a USB drive. Do this before
    anything else, because the reset in the next step deletes everything
    locally.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 2: deactivate and factory reset</h2>

  <p>
    Go to Settings, Users and Accounts, Other, then Console Sharing and Offline
    Play, and disable it. That removes the console as your primary device so
    the new owner cannot access your purchases. Then go to Settings, System,
    Reset Options, and choose Reset Your Console. This wipes your account and
    all data and returns the PS5 to the out-of-box setup screen.
  </p>

  <p>
    Sign out of your account on any related apps too, like the PlayStation App
    linked to the console. It is an easy step to forget, but leaving a device
    linked to your account is the most common way people get locked out later.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 3: clean it and gather the extras</h2>

  <p>
    A clean console sells faster and for more than a dusty one. Wipe the body
    with a dry or barely damp cloth, blow out the vents with canned air, and
    clean the controller, especially the grips and the gap around the sticks.
    Gather the HDMI cable, power cable, at least one controller, and the
    original box if you still have it. The box alone signals to buyers that the
    console was cared for, and it genuinely bumps the price.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 4: price it to sell</h2>

  <p>
    Check what similar consoles have actually sold for before you set a number.
    On eBay, search sold listings rather than active ones, because asking
    prices are not selling prices. In mid-2026 a disc PS5 in good condition with
    one controller and the box realistically sells for about $350 on a good
    day, and digital models about $40 to $60 less. If you include games, add
    their used value on top. Price slightly under the sold average if you want
    it gone in a week.
  </p>

  <h2>Step 5: choose where to sell</h2>

  <p>
    Facebook Marketplace usually gets the highest net price because there are
    no platform fees, but it means meeting strangers and dealing with
    lowballers. eBay reaches more buyers and has seller protections, but takes
    a cut. Swappa is a good middle ground for tech, with lower fees than eBay
    and a gamer-focused audience. GameStop trade-in is the floor: fast and safe,
    but it pays noticeably less than any private sale.
  </p>

  <p>
    Whichever you pick, take clear photos in good light, show the console
    powered on with the home screen visible, and describe any scratches
    honestly. A listing with a photo of the console actually running sells
    faster and returns fewer complaints.
  </p>

  <h2>When a trade-in is the better move</h2>

  <p>
    Skip the private sale if your console has a fault, like a noisy fan or a
    drifting controller, that you would rather not disclose in detail. A
    trade-in service will still take it, and you avoid the risk of a buyer
    disputing the sale later. Skip it too if you need the money same day. The
    private route earns more, but it can take a week or two, and the extra $50
    to $100 is not worth the wait when you are in a hurry.
  </p>
</ArticleLayout>
